WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.610
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:11.220
Binaural or 2.5D audio means a sound recording
that provides the listener with an amazing

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.220
3D-ish sound sensation.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.920
It produces a sound that feels highly realistic
when listened to through headphones, and therefore,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.050
using a pair is highly recommended for this
episode.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:27.749
It sounds way more immersive than regular
mono, or even stereo audio signals, but also

00:00:27.749 --> 00:00:32.920
requires more expertise to produce, and is
therefore quite scarce on the internet.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:53.710
Let’s listen to the difference together.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.290
We have not only heard sound samples here,
but could also see the accompanying video

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:03.300
content which reveals the position of the
players and the composition of the scene in

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.199
which the recording is made.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:11.729
This sounds like a perfect fit for an AI to
take piece of mono audio and use this additional

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:15.560
information to convert it to make it sound
binaural.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.350
This project is exactly about that, where
a deep convolutional neural network is used

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:26.939
to look at both the video and the single-channel
audio content in our footage, and then, predict

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:31.520
what it would have sounded like were it recorded
as a binaural signal.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:02:28.670
Let’s listen to a few results.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:33.250
The fact that we can use the visual content
as well as the audio with this neural network,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:43.900
interestingly, also enables us to separate
the sound of an instrument within the mix.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:54.170
Let’s listen.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:03:04.939
To validate the results, the authors both
used a quantitative, mathematical way of comparing

00:03:04.939 --> 00:03:10.230
their results to the ground truth, and not
only that, but they also carried out two user

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.959
studies as well.

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:17.490
In the first one, the ground truth was shown
to the users and they were asked to judge,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.819
which of the two techniques were better.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:25.920
In this study, this new method performed better
than previous methods, and in the second setup,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.620
users were asked to name the directions they
hear the different instrument sounds coming

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:31.620
from.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.940
In this case, the new method outperformed
the previous techniques by a significant margin,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:42.750
and if we keep progressing like this, we may
be at most a couple papers away from 2.5D

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:47.250
audio synthesis that sounds indistinguishable
from the real deal.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:52.019
Looking forward to a future where we can enjoy
all kinds of video content with this kind

00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:53.019
of immersion.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:56.760
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

